		"x402_batch_settlement": {
			"live": true,
			"post_date": "2026-05-11 (updated 2026-06-24)",
			"authors": "Cam Whiteside (Cloudflare), Carson Roscoe, Conner Swenberg, Josh Nickerson, Philippe d'Argent (Coinbase)",
			"paragraph": "TL;DR: The x402 protocol is introducing batch settlement, enabling agents to transact at extremely low latency and fractions of a cent. Agents provide cryptographic vouchers that enable sellers to verify payment intent without an onchain transfer at every step. Batch settlement separates intent-to-pay from onchain finality: at request time the buyer provides a proof of authorization that is near-instant to verify; at scale, value moves onchain only when it is economically optimal, amortized across hundreds or thousands of interactions. This is a core protocol scheme, not a vendor-specific extension. The 402 header advertises a ceiling per interaction; the seller captures only actual usage against the batch escrow. The dominant expense shifts toward the session or day rather than the individual call."
		}
